              "implementationGuidance": [
                {
                  "id": "AID-R-003.001-G001",
                  "implementation": "Require MFA or step-up authentication for all restored sessions.",
                  "howTo": "<h5>Purpose and boundary</h5><p>Use this identity-provider integration reference architecture to verify that every applicable restored interactive session was created only after the policy-required MFA or step-up event and is bound to a fresh post-reset authentication context. It does not rotate workload credentials or prove authorization for tools.</p><h5>Sign the session-reset and authentication policy</h5><p>The identity recovery authority signs the incident, tenant and application scope, exact affected-principal and revoked-session population, reset cutoff, allowed factor and assurance classes, required token claims, IdP tenant/policy versions, exempt non-interactive identity classes, evidence freshness, and exception policy. Factor strength and reauthentication windows come only from that versioned policy.</p><pre><code class=\"language-yaml\"># File: recovery/contracts/session-step-up-policy.yaml\nschema_version: aidefend.r003.session-step-up-policy.v1\nrequired_bindings:\n  - system_or_tenant\n  - incident_id\n  - recovery_release_id\n  - reset_cutoff_at\n  - affected_principal_manifest_digest\n  - revoked_session_manifest_digest\n  - idp_policy_digest\n  - allowed_factor_classes\n  - required_acr_values\n  - required_amr_rules\n  - exception_policy_digest\nreceipt_required_fields:\n  - expected_principal_count\n  - observed_principal_count\n  - expected_new_session_count\n  - verified_new_session_count\n  - nonconforming_session_ids\n  - unmeasured_principal_ids\n  - result\n</code></pre><h5>Enforce and retain evidence</h5><p>Configure MFA/step-up at the IdP or authorization server, not by trusting an application header. Revoke pre-cutoff sessions, require a fresh authentication transaction, validate issuer, audience, signature, nonce, <code>auth_time</code>, <code>acr</code>, and <code>amr</code>, then issue a new session under a recovery release identifier. The owner retains signed policy exports, revocation receipts, authentication events, token-claim digests, exceptions, counts, and a detached-signed result in retention-locked evidence; do not retain raw factors or reusable tokens.</p><h5>Independent readback</h5><p>A read-only verifier outside the IdP-policy owner trust domain derives the expected principals from the signed population, reads revocation and authentication events directly from the IdP, verifies a policy-defined complete new-session population, and performs positive/negative login tests for every applicable assurance branch. Dependencies are the signed affected-principal/session manifests, IdP trust roots, enforceable policy, trusted time, complete authentication telemetry, and evidence storage. Revocation success is input eligibility, never this guidance's <code>PASS</code>.</p><p><strong>Action:</strong> authorize restored interactive sessions only from independently verified post-reset IdP evidence; a successful application login alone is not MFA proof.</p>"
                }
              ]
